      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lince was officially created as a district on May 18 1936 by Legislative Resolution 8281, making it one of Lima's newest administrative units.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.gob.pe/13530-historia-del-distrito",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Lince’s western side, known as Lobatón, and its eastern side, divided by Avenida Arenales, form the two main sections of the neighborhood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lince_District",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the late 1930s, many art‑deco buildings were built in Lince, and most still retain their original design with little alteration.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lince_District",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Before Spanish conquest, the area that is now Lince was part of the curacazgo of Guatca, a fertile agricultural zone that grew maize, cotton, and sugarcane.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.gob.pe/13530-historia-del-distrito",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The ancient Guatca aqueduct carried water from the Rímac River to irrigate fields that later became Lince, San Isidro and Miraflores.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
